Q: Is there a way to specify how many times a reference to captured group must be repeated in the replacement string? A: <p>Using <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.str-repeat.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">str_repeat</a> works to prepare the the replacement string to be like <code>&quot;$1$1&quot;</code> for <a href="https://www.php.net/manual/en/function.preg-replace.php" rel="nofollow noreferrer">preg_replace</a>.</p>
<p>But there might be a catch here if this is not for a single character because:</p>
<p><em>Repeating a capture group will only capture the value of the last iteration.</em></p>
<p>So in this case it works for a single character <code>Z</code>, but if you want to match multiple repetitions of ABC like this with a capture group <code>(ABC){3,}</code></p>
<p>The output might be unexpected:</p>
<pre><code>$billing = &quot;ABCABCABC&quot;;
$repetitions = 2;

$result = preg_replace('/(ABC){3,}/', str_repeat(&quot;$1&quot;, $repetitions), $billing);

echo $result; // ABCABC

</code></pre>
<p>Note that you don't need a capture group, you can use <code>$0</code> for the full match and repeat that:</p>
<pre><code>$billing = &quot;ABCABCABC&quot;;
$repetitions = 2;

$result = preg_replace('/(?:ABC){3,}/', str_repeat(&quot;$0&quot;, $repetitions), $billing);

echo $result; // ABCABCABCABCABCABC
</code></pre>
<p>Or with a callback:</p>
<pre><code>$result = preg_replace_callback(
    '/(?:ABC){3,}/',
    fn($m) =&gt; str_repeat($m[0], $repetitions),
    $billing
);
</code></pre>

Q: Can I determine if an argument is string literal? A: <p>C++26 introduces <code>std::is_string_literal</code> from <code>&lt;meta&gt;</code> exactly for this!</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;meta&gt;

int main()
{
    static_assert(std::is_string_literal(&quot;foo&quot;));
}
</code></pre>
<p>Available in GCC-15 and GCC-16-trunk</p>
<p>If you want to use it with older standards, use <code>__builtin_is_string_literal</code> which is underlying compiler builtin used to implement this function.</p>

Q: Removing unsafe pointer and make vector element comparison parallel A: <blockquote>
<p>Is it possible to implement a solution that does not use <code>unsafe</code> pointers without making copies of the <code>Vec&lt;B&gt;</code> or what it contains?</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Is there a way to modify <code>elem_j</code> the same way <code>elem_i</code> is modified if <code>criterion</code> is met? For now the loop goes through the whole <code>Vec</code> for each elements.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Yes, by splitting <code>B</code>.</strong></p>
<p>A common pattern in Rust is to split data-structures based on access patterns. Here, this means cutting out the <code>nb</code> field out of <code>B</code>. Then <code>elems</code> can be iterated immutably, so there's no borrowing issues any longer. <a href="https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&amp;mode=debug&amp;edition=2024&amp;gist=d159f2f06e626c2bd8aa54f11b8f8d1f" rel="nofollow noreferrer">See Playground</a>.</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>struct B {
    r: Vec&lt;f64&gt;,
    v: Vec&lt;f64&gt;,
    f: Vec&lt;f64&gt;,
}

struct A {
    elems: Vec&lt;B&gt;,
    links: Vec&lt;Vec&lt;usize&gt;&gt;,
    cutoff: f64,
}
</code></pre>
<p>And then the list building can be switched to:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>    pub fn build_links(&amp;mut self) {
        self.links.iter_mut().for_each(|links| links.clear());

        self.elems
            .iter()
            .enumerate()
            .for_each(|(i, elem_i)| {
                let offset = i + 1;

                self.elems[offset..]
                    .iter()
                    .enumerate()
                    .for_each(|(j, elem_j)| {
                        if elem_i.criterion(&amp;elem_j) &lt; self.cutoff {
                            self.links[i].push(offset + j);
                            self.links[offset + j].push(i);
                        }
                    });
            });

        self.links.iter_mut().for_each(|links| links.sort_unstable());
    }
</code></pre>
<p>I took the liberty to make the inner iteration triangular, as you had mentioned in the text, avoiding duplicate tests on i/j pairs since the <code>criterion</code> test is symmetric.</p>
<p>If you really <code>nb</code> to be part of <code>B</code>, this can still be accommodated with a bit extra work before/after. <a href="https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&amp;mode=debug&amp;edition=2024&amp;gist=ccf35858dedff6cb34a140943799108c" rel="nofollow noreferrer">See Playground</a></p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>    pub fn build_links(&amp;mut self) {
        //  The buffer could be reused by storing it in A, and using `Vec::extend`.
        let mut nbs: Vec&lt;_&gt; = self
            .elems
            .iter_mut()
            .map(|elem| {
                elem.nb.clear();

                std::mem::take(&amp;mut elem.nb)
            })
            .collect();

        self.elems
            .iter()
            .enumerate()
            .for_each(|(i, elem_i)| {
                let offset = i + 1;

                self.elems[offset..]
                    .iter()
                    .enumerate()
                    .for_each(|(j, elem_j)| {
                        if elem_i.criterion(&amp;elem_j) &lt; self.cutoff {
                            nbs[i].push(offset + j);
                            nbs[offset + j].push(i);
                        }
                    });
            });

        nbs.into_iter()
            .zip(&amp;mut self.elems)
            .for_each(|(mut nb, elem)| {
                nb.sort_unstable();
                elem.nb = nb;
            });
    }
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>How can we implement a parallel version of this? I tried to use <code>par_iter_mut</code> from rayon but it tells me that <code>* const B</code> does not implement <code>Sync</code> and <code>Send</code>. Is there another library I could use.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The triangular form cannot be parallelized, as is, because two concurrent threads could attempt to mutate <code>nbs[i]</code> at the same time.</p>
<p>You can go back to the linear pass, and you'll be able to parallelize (thanks to <code>zip</code>), but you'll be doing twice the work:</p>
<pre class="lang-rust prettyprint-override"><code>        self.elems
            .iter()
            .zip(&amp;mut nbs)
            .enumerate()
            .for_each(|(i, (elem_i, nb_i))| {
                self.elems
                    .iter()
                    .enumerate()
                    .for_each(|(j, elem_j)| {
                        if i != j &amp;&amp; elem_i.criterion(&amp;elem_j) &lt; self.cutoff {
                            nbs_i.push(j);
                        }
                    });
            });
</code></pre>
<p>Or, you could still do the triangular iteration, then have a post-pass which duplicates the indexes -- ie, if for <code>j</code> in each of <code>elem[i].nb</code> push <code>i</code> to <code>elem[j].nb</code>.</p>
<p>Depending on how dense/sparse <code>nb</code> is, either method could pull ahead.</p>
